  {
   "id": "republic",
   "text": "At first, Rome was ruled by kings. Then, according to Roman tradition, in five hundred nine BC, the Romans drove out their last king, Tarquin the Proud, and vowed never to have another. Instead, citizens elected two consuls every year, guided by a Senate of powerful elders. The Roman Republic was born.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "era",
    "year": "509 BC",
    "title": "The Republic Is Born",
    "sub": "No more kings",
    "icons": [
     "lib:kings-033",
     "fluent-3d:classical-building",
     "lib:soldiers-159"
    ],
    "cue": "five"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "italy",
   "text": "Step by step, Rome took over Italy. It captured the Etruscan city of Veii in three ninety-six BC and fought long wars in the southern mountains. It built the Appian Way, starting in three twelve BC, so its armies could march fast to Capua. When the Greek city of Tarentum called in King Pyrrhus, with his war elephants, Rome lost battles but won the war. By two seventy-two BC, it ruled most of Italy.",
